.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.sp
\fB[block]\fP
.RS 4
The settings of the "[block]" section will be set on \fBall\fP block devices
found in \fI/sys/block\fP, which are considered as \fBvalid\fP.
.br
\~
.br
The following rules apply for \fBvalid\fP devices:
.br
\~
.br
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
all multipath devices (\fIdm\-*\fP, if_ mpath\-\fI, but not _LVM\-\fP or other \fIdm\-\fP)
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
all physical disks (indicated by \fIdevice/type=0\fP or names like \fInvme*\fP,
\fIvd*\fP; for \fInvme*\fP devices additional check for existence of file
\*(Aqqueue/scheduler\*(Aq and \*(Aqqueue/nr_requests\*(Aq)
.br
\fBexcept\fP they are part of a device mapper construct (like \fImpath\-\fP).
.RE
.sp
\~
.br
The section \fB[block]\fP can contain the following options:
.br
.sp
\fBIO_SCHEDULER=\fP\fISTRING\fP
.RS 4
The default I/O scheduler for single\-queued block layer devices offers
satisfactory performance for wide range of I/O task, however choosing
an alternative scheduler may potentially yield better latency
characteristics and throughput. "noop" is an alternative scheduler, in
comparison to other schedulers it may offer more consistent
performance, lower computation overhead, and potentially higher
throughput. For most SAP environments (RAID, storage arrays,
virtualization) \*(Aqnoop\*(Aq is the better choice.
.br
With the new introduced multi\-queue scheduler for block layer devices
the recommended I/O scheduler is \*(Aqnone\*(Aq as an equivalent to \*(Aqnoop\*(Aq for
single\-queued block layer devices.
.br
\~
.br
So \fBIO_SCHEDULER\fP can now contain a comma separated list of possible
schedulers, which are checked from left to right. The first one which
is available in \fI/sys/block/<device>/queue/scheduler\fP will be used as
new scheduler setting for the respective block device.
.br
The selection per device is logged.
.br
When set, \fBall\fP block devices on the system will be switched to one of
the chosen schedulers.
.br
Valid values can be found in \fI/sys/block/<device>/queue/scheduler\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fBNRREQ=\fP\fIINT\fP
.RS 4
IO \fBnr_requests\fP specifies the maximum number of read and write requests
that can be queued at one time. The default value is 128, which means
that 128 read requests and 128 write requests can be queued before the
next process to request a read or write is put to sleep.
.br
When set, the number of requests for \fBall\fP block devices on the system
will be switched to the chosen value.
.RE
.sp
\fBREAD_AHEAD_KB=\fP\fIINT\fP
.RS 4
disk readahead (\fIqueue/read_ahead_kb\fP) defines the maximum number of
kilobytes that the operating system may read ahead during a sequential
read operation. As a result, the likely\-needed information is already
present within the kernel page cache for the next sequential read,
which improves read I/O performance. Device mappers often benefit from
a high read_ahead_kb value. Increasing the read_ahead_kb value might
improve performance in environments where sequential reading of large
files takes place.
.br
When set, the value of read_ahead_kb for \fBall\fP block devices on the
system will be switched to the chosen value.
.RE
.sp
\fBMAX_SECTORS_KB=\fP\fIINT\fP
.RS 4
disk max_sectors_kb (\fIqueue/max_sectors_kb\fP) defines the maximum number
of kilobytes that the block layer will allow for a filesystem request.
Must be smaller than or equal to the maximum size allowed by the
hardware (\fIqueue/max_hw_sectors_kb\fP).
.br
When set, the value of \fBmax_sectors_kb\fP for \fBall\fP block devices on the
system will be switched to the chosen value.
.br
If the value is higher than \fBmax_hw_sectors_kb\fP it will be limited to
\fBmax_hw_sectors_kb\fP and a footnote is displayed.
.RE
.RE
.sp
\fB[cpu]\fP
.RS 4
The section "[cpu]" manipulates files in \fI/sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu*\fP.
.br
This section can only contain the following options:
.sp
\fBenergy_perf_bias=\fP\fISTRING\fP
.RS 4
Energy Performance Bias EPB (applies to Intel\-based systems only)
.br
supported values are: \fBperformance\fP (0), \fBnormal\fP (6) and \fBpowersave\fP
(15)
.br
The command \fBcpupower set \-b <value>\fP is used to set the value, if the
system supports Intel\(cqs performance bias setting. See \fBcpupower\fP(1) and
\fBcpupower\-set\fP(1) for more information.
.br
If system does not support Intel\(cqs performance bias setting \-
\*(Aq\fBall:none\fP\*(Aq is used in the column \*(Aq\fIActual\fP\*(Aq of the verify table and
the \fIfootnote\fP \*(Aq[1] setting is not supported by the system\*(Aq is
displayed.
.br
\~
.br
When set as \fBenergy_perf_bias=<performance|normal|powersave\fP in the
Note definition file, the value will be set for \fBall\fP available
CPUs.
.br
The command \fBcpupower \-c all set \-b <value>\fP or \fBcpupower \-c <cpu>
set \-b <value>\fP is used to set the value.
.RE
.sp
\fBgovernor=\fP\fISTRING\fP
.RS 4
CPU Frequency/Voltage scaling (applies to Intel\-based systems only)
.br
The clock frequency and voltage of modern CPUs can scale, in order to
save energy when there\(cqs less work to be done. However SAP HANA as a
high\-performance database benefits from high CPU frequencies.
.br
Supported values are: \fBperformance\fP and \fBpowersave\fP
.br
The command \fBcpupower frequency\-set \-g <value>\fP is used to set the
value, if the value is a supported governor. See \fBcpupower\fP(1) and
\fBcpupower\-frequency\-set\fP(1) for more information.
.br
If the governor settings of all available CPUs are equal,
\*(Aq\fBall:<governor>\fP\*(Aq is used in the column \*(Aq\fIActual\fP\*(Aq of the verify
table. If not, each CPU with its assigned governor is listed (e.g.
\fBcpu1:powersave cpu2:powersave cpu3:powersave cpu4:powersave\fP
\fBcpu5:powersave cpu6:powersave cpu7:powersave cpu0:performance\fP)
.br
\~
.br
When set as \fBgovernor=<performance|powersave\fP in the Note definition
file, the value will be set for \fBall\fP available CPUs.
.br
The command \fBcpupower \-c all frequency\-set \-g <value>\fP or \fBcpupower
\-c <cpu> frequency\-set \-g <value>\fP is used to set the value.
.RE
.sp
\fBforce_latency=\fP\fISTRING\fP
.RS 4
force latency \- configure C\-States for lower latency (applies to
Intel\-based systems only)
.br
Input is a valid C\-State name supported by the system, see
\fI/sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu0/cpuidle/state*/name\fP for valid values.
.br
This C\-State name is internally mapped into an integer value
representing the related latency value, see
\fI/sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu0/cpuidle/state*/latency\fP
.br
OR
.br
Input is a string, which is internally treated as a decimal (not a
hexadecimal) integer number representing a maximum response time in
microseconds.
.br
It is used to establish a latency upper limit by limiting the use of
C\-States (CPU idle or CPU latency states) to only those with an exit
latency smaller than the value set here. That means only those states
that require less than the requested number of microseconds to wake up
are enabled, all the other C\-States are disabled.
.br
The files \fI/sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu*/cpuidle/state*/latency\fP and
\fI/sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu*/cpuidle/state*/disable\fP are used to
limit the C\-States.
.br
\~
.br
If system does not support force latency settings \- \fBall:none\fP is
used in the column \*(Aq\fIActual\fP\*(Aq of the verify table and the \fIfootnote\fP
\*(Aq[1] setting is not supported by the system\*(Aq is displayed.
.br
\~
.br
When set in the Note definition file for all available CPUs all CPU
latency states with a value read from
\fI/sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu*/cpuidle/state*/latency\fP \fB>=\fP (higher
than) the value from the Note definition file are disabled by writing
\*(Aq\fB1\fP\*(Aq to \fI/sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu*/cpuidle/state*/disable\fP
.br
\~
.br
ATTENTION: not idling \fBat all\fP increases power consumption
significantly and reduces the life span of the machine because of wear
and tear. So do not use a too strict latency setting. For SAP HANA
workloads a value of \*(Aq\fB70\fP\*(Aq microseconds (as a "light sleep") seems to
be sufficient. And the impact on power consumption and life of the
CPUs is less severe. But don\(cqt forget: The deeper the idle state, the
larger is the exit latency.
.RE
.RE
.sp
\fB[filesystem]\fP
.RS 4
The section \fB[filesystem]\fP is checking filesystem mount options.
.br
The values from the Note definition files are only checked against
\fI/proc/mounts\fP and \fI/etc/fstab\fP. Changing the filesystem mount options
is not supported by \fBsaptune\fP.
.br
\~
.br
This section can only contain the following parameter:
.sp
\fBxfs_options=\fP\fISTRING\fP
.RS 4
where \fBSTRING\fP is a list of valid mount options separated by \fB,\fP.
.br
A prefix \fB\-\fP for the option indicates, that the option should NOT be
available on any xfs filesystem. A prefix \fB+\fP or no prefix for the
option indicates, that the option should be available on any xfs
filesystem.
.br
\~
.br
For the check first the \fBmounted\fP filesystems of the requested
filesystem type (for now only xfs) will be read from \fI/proc/mounts\fP
and separated in a list with mount points containing the option and
another list with mount points NOT containing the option.
.br
Then the defined filesystems of the requested filesystem type (for now
only xfs) will be read from \fI/etc/fstab\fP, skipping the already
mounted mount points and split the remaining entries in a list with
mount points containing the option and another list with mount points
NOT containing the option.
.br
At least combine the lists from proc and fstab to get one list of
mount points containing the option and another list with mount points
NOT containing the option.
.br
\~
.br
To decide, if a mount point contains the option or not, we use a
simple string comparison between the value from the Note definition
file and the option available on the system.
.br
\fBThis can lead to a not\-compliant result even everything is in
order!\fP
.br
Because default options might not appear in the output of
\fI/proc/mounts\fP, they are not found even if they are set internally. Also
the content of the the \*(Aqdefaults\*(Aq entry of not\-mounted filesystem is
opaque.
.br
Keep this in mind when crafting Overrides or extra Notes!
.RE
.RE
.sp
\fB[grub]\fP
.RS 4
The section \fB[grub]\fP is checking kernel command line settings for grub.
The values from the Note definition files are only checked against
\fI/proc/cmdline\fP. Changing the grub configuration is not supported by
\fBsaptune\fP.
.br
\~
.br
Some of these values are set by \*(Aqalternative\*(Aq settings by \fBsaptune\fP during
runtime, so changing the grub configuration is possible but not needed.
.br
\~
.br
This section can contain options like:
.sp
\fBintel_idle.max_cstate=1\fP and \fBprocessor.max_cstate=1\fP
.RS 4
Configure C\-States for lower latency in Linux (applies to Intel\-based
systems only) \- see force_latency in section \fB[cpu]\fP as \*(Aqalternative\*(Aq
settings
.RE
.sp
\fBnuma_balancing=disable\fP
.RS 4
Turn off autoNUMA balancing \- see \fBkernel.numa_balancing\fP in section
[sysctl] as \*(Aqalternative\*(Aq settings
.RE
.sp
\fBtransparent_hugepage=never\fP
.RS 4
Configure transparent hugepages \- see THP in section \fB[vm]\fP as
\*(Aqalternative\*(Aq settings
.RE
.RE
\" _strm_3.2.0_start
.sp
\fB[limits]\fP \fBATTENTION:\fP deprecated
.RS 4
The section "[limits]" is dealing with ulimit settings for user login
sessions in the pam_limits module. The settings will \fBNOT\fP be done in
the central limits file \fI/etc/security/limits.conf\fP. Instead there will
be a \fBdrop\-in file\fP in \fI/etc/security/limits.d\fP for each
domain\-item\-type combination used in the Note definition file.
.br
\~
.br
The drop\-in file name syntax will be:
.br
\fIsaptune\-<domain>\-<item>\-<type>.conf\fP
.br
\~
.br
For more information and a description of the syntax and the needed
fields please look at \fBlimits.conf\fP(5).
.br
\~
.br
This section has to contain the following option:
.sp
\fBLIMITS=\fP\fISTRING\fP
.RS 4
where STR*ING is a list of valid limit definitions separated by \fB,\fP
.br
a valid limit definition contains the fields \*(Aqdomain item type value\*(Aq
separated by one space.
.br
For more information about the syntax of valid limit definitions
please refer to \fBlimits.conf\fP(5) or the comment section of
\fI/etc/security/limits.conf\fP.
.br
Note: The \fB@\fP sign in front of the domain name matches a group.
.br
\~
.br
To leave \fBall\fP limits definitions of a Note definition file
\*(Aquntouched\*(Aq in the system, leave the \fBLIMITS\fP string in the \fBoverride
file\fP of the Note definition file empty
.br
\~
.br
To leave only \fBsome\fP of the limits definitions of a Note definition
file \*(Aquntouched\*(Aq in the system, remove these limits definitions from
the \fBLIMITS\fP string in the \fBoverride file\fP of the Note definition
file.
.RE
.RE
.sp
\fB[login]\fP
.RS 4
The section \fB[login]\fP manipulates the behavior of the systemd login
manager.
.br
This section can \fBonly\fP contain the following option:
.sp
\fBUserTasksMax=\fP\fISTRING\fP
.RS 4
This option is only available on SLE12. In SLE15 the limit is removed
from the systemd login manager and therefore the setting is no longer
supported by \fBsaptune\fP.
.br
\~
.br
This option configures a parameter of the systemd login manager. It
sets the maximum number of OS tasks each user may run concurrently.
The behavior of the systemd login manager was changed starting
SLES12SP2 to prevent fork bomb attacks.
.br
\~
.br
Recommended value is \fBinfinity\fP.
.br
\~
.br
If set, the drop\-in file
\fI/etc/systemd/logind.conf.d/saptune\-UserTasksMax.conf\fP is created and
for all currently logged in users the maximum number of OS tasks each
user may run concurrently is changed using the command \fBsystemctl
\-\-runtime set\-property user\-<uid>.slice TasksMax=<value>\fP.
.br
After creating the drop\-in file the \fBsystemd\-logind.service\fP will be
reloaded.
.br
\~
.br
ATTENTION: With this setting your system is vulnerable to fork bomb
attacks
.RE
.RE
\" _strm_3.2.0_end
.sp
\fB[mem]\fP
.RS 4
The section "[mem]" manipulates the size of TMPFS (\fI/dev/shm\fP).
.br
\~
.br
With the STD implementation, the SAP Extended Memory is no longer stored
in the TMPFS (under \fI/dev/shm\fP). However, the TMPFS is required by the
Virtual Machine Container (VMC). For this reason, we still recommend the
same configuration of the TMPFS:
.br
75% (RAM + Swap) is still recommended as the size.
.br
This section can contain the following options:
.sp
\fBShmFileSystemSizeMB=\fP\fIINT\fP
.RS 4
Use \fBShmFileSystemSizeMB\fP to set an absolute value for your TMPFS.
.br
If \fBShmFileSystemSizeMB\fP is set to a value > 0, the setting for
\fBVSZ_TMPFS_PERCENT\fP will be ignored and the size will NOT be
calculated.
.br
If \fBShmFileSystemSizeMB\fP is set to \fB0\fP the size will be calculated
using \fBVSZ_TMPFS_PERCENT\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fBVSZ_TMPFS_PERCENT=\fP\fIINT\fP
.RS 4
Size of TMPFS mounted on \fI/dev/shm\fP in percent of the virtual
memory.
.br
Depending on the size of the virtual memory (physical+swap) the value
is calculated by \fB(RAM + SWAP) * VSZ_TMPFS_PERCENT/100\fP.
.br
If \fBVSZ_TMPFS_PERCENT\fP is set to \*(Aq\fB0\fP\*(Aq, the value is calculated by (RAM
+ SWAP) * 75/100, as the default is 75.
+
As this parameter is only used to calculate the value of
\fBShmFileSystemSizeMB\fP it will not be checked and compared during the
saptune operation \*(Aqverify\*(Aq. A footnote is pointing this out.
.RE
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.br
Newer \fBcloud\-init\fP versions have a dependency of \fBcloud\-final.service\fP
to \fImulti\-user.target\fP which prevents \fBsaptune\fP to run on boot on Azure
systems if the systemd service drop\-in file
\fI/etc/systemd/system/saptune.service.d/10\-after_cloud\-init.conf\fP is
created by \fBsaptune\fP.
.br
With 3.2.3 on each execution \fBsaptune\fP will \fBnot\fP create this file
anymore, but remove it if present!
.br
Additionally SAP Note \fB941735\fP contains a \fB[mem:csp=azure]\fP section with
\fBdisabled\fP ShmFileSystemSizeMB and \fBVSZ_TMPFS_PERCENT\fP parameters
because calculating the size of \fI/dev/shm\fP is not reliable possible
anymore if swap is configured by \fBcloud\-init\fP. This can lead to
sporadic non\-compliant verify reports.
.br
If you really still need to change the size of TMPFS for you SAP
workload, please do it by \fBother\fP means.
.br
If you nevertheless prefer to use \fBsaptune\fP to set the size of TMPFS
\fBand\fP if you have a \fBpersistent\fP swap size during boot you can use an
override file for Note \fB941735\fP to set your desired TMPFS parameter
settings.
.RE
\" _strm_3.2.0_start
.sp
\fB[pagecache]\fP
.RS 4
The section \fB[pagecache]\fP is dealing with the pagecache limit feature as
described in SAP Note 1557506, which is only available on SLE12.
.br
\~
.br
ATTENTION: The pagecache limit Note will \fBNOT\fP be part of any Solution
definition by default. As it is essential to configure this feature
really carefully, you need to customize the Note definition file first
to enable the feature and then you can apply the Note settings manually.
After that, the settings will be applied automatically during each
startup of the system.
.br
This section can contain the following options:
.sp
\fBENABLE_PAGECACHE_LIMIT=yes|no\fP
.RS 4
This defines whether pagecache limit feature should be enabled or not.
It is a yesno value. By default it is set to \fBno\fP
.br
Consider to enable pagecache limit feature if your SAP workloads cause
frequent and excessive swapping activities. It is recommended to leave
pagecache limit disabled if the system has low or no swap space.
.RE
.sp
\fBvm.pagecache_limit_ignore_dirty=\fP\fIINT\fP
.RS 4
Whether or not to ignore dirty memory when enforcing the pagecache
limit.
.br
If set to 0, dirty memory will be freed (written onto disk) when
enforcing the pagecache limit.
.br
If set to 1 (default), dirty memory will not be freed when enforcing
the pagecache limit.
.br
If set to 2 \- a middle ground, some dirty memory will be freed when
enforcing the limit.
.RE
.sp
\fBOVERRIDE_PAGECACHE_LIMIT_MB=\fP\fIINT\fP
.RS 4
When pagecache limit feature is enabled, the limit value is usually
automatically calculated using the \*(AqSAP HANA formula\*(Aq, which means 2% of
system memory is used as pagecache limit.
.br
However, the value can be overridden if you set this parameter to the
desired limit value.
.br
To remove the override, set the parameter to empty string.
.RE
.RE
\" _strm_3.2.0_end
.sp
\fB[reminder]\fP
.RS 4
The section \fB[reminder]\fP contains important information and all settings
of a SAP Note, which can not be set by \fBsaptune\fP.
.br
\~
.br
This section is displayed at the end of the \fBsaptune\fP options \*(Aqverify\*(Aq,
\*(Aqsimulate\*(Aq and \*(Aqapply\*(Aq. It will be highlighted with red color to get the
attention of the customer.
.br
\~
.br
Sometimes this section may include lines with parameter settings
commented out as the SAP Note only contains rough estimations as the
settings are highly customer environment and workload dependent. Please
be aware that these parameter settings can\(cqt be activated by an override
file. If you need to set such parameters you need to create a \*(Aqcustom\*(Aq
Note containing these settings by using \fBsaptune note create\fP
.RE
.sp
\fB[rpm]\fP
.RS 4
The section \fB[rpm]\fP is checking rpm versions on the system. The values
from the Note definition files are only checked against the installed
rpm versions on the system. No other action is supported.
.br
Only \fBlower\fP versions installed on the system are considered as
non\-compliant. A installed rpm version equal or higher is considered as
compliant.
.br
Package dependencies \- if needed \- are handled by the \fBsaptune\fP package
installation.
.br
\~
.br
Additional to the check for mandatory packages we now (starting with
version 3.2.2) support the check of \fIoptional\fP packages.
.br
A \fB?\fP prefix as the first character of the package name indicates an
\fIoptional\fP package. \fIOptional\fP packages have to follow the \fBNew\fP Syntax
described below.
.br
The version compliance check will only be done if the package is
installed. In this case it must follow the version restriction described
above.
.br
Additional it is possible to omit the version for \fIoptional\fP packages.
In this case each installed version is compliant. It\(cqs a way to list if
an optional package is at least available on the system.
.br
During the \*(Aqverify\*(Aq and \*(Aqsimulate\*(Aq operation an \fIoptional\fP package is
only listed if the package is installed. The \fB?\fP prefix is displayed in
the parameter output as \fBrpm:?PACKAGENAME\fP.
.br
\~
.br
With the availability of tagged sections, we support 2 different types
of rpm line syntax. The first one \- our \fBOld\fP Syntax \- only for
compatibility reasons. The second one \- our \fBNew\fP Syntax \- is our
preferred syntax in combination with tagged rpm sections.
.br
\~
.br
\fBOld\fP Syntax:
.br
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
<rpm package name> <SLE Version> <rpm package version>
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.sp
this syntax is mainly used for compatibility reasons and when using a
\*(Aqnon\-tagged\*(Aq rpm section.
.br
Add one line for each SLE version a package should be checked for, even
if the package version is the same.
.br
The SLE version has to be noted in the same format as the \*(Aq\fBVERSION=\fP\*(Aq
entry in \fI/etc/os\-release\fP.
.br
\~
.br
To address all SLE versions and service packs the keyword \*(Aq\fBall\fP\*(Aq can be
used instead of a dedicated SLE version.
.br
\~
.br
e.g
.br
systemd 12\-SP2 228\-142.1
.br
sapinit\-systemd\-compat 12 1.0\-2.1
.br
util\-linux 16.0 2.40.4\-160000.1.3
.br
bzip2 all 1.0.8
.br
\~
.br
Only the lines where the SLE version is matching the running system OS
are checked and displayed during the \*(Aqverify\*(Aq and \*(Aqsimulate\*(Aq option.
.br
Entries with non\-matching SLE versions are reported in the \fBsaptune\fP log
file as INFO
.br
That means, if there is no matching SLE version for the running OS no
rpm entries are listed during the \*(Aqverify\*(Aq and \*(Aqsimulate\*(Aq operation.
.br
\~
.br
\fBNew\fP Syntax:
.br
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
<rpm package name> <rpm package version>
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.sp
This syntax is the preferred syntax when using a \*(Aqtagged\*(Aq rpm section,
where the targeted operating system and/or system architecture is
defined by using the tags \fBos=\fP and/or \fBarch=\fP
.br
Add one line for each package and package version to be checked. Only
\fIoptional\fP packages are allowed to omit the package version.
.br
\~
.br
e.g
.br
systemd 228\-142.1
.br
util\-linux 2.25\-22.1
.br
?sssd 2.9.3\-150600.3.25.1
.br
?polkit
.br
\~
.br
Only the lines where the tags of the section match the running system OS
and/or the system architecture are checked and displayed during the
\*(Aqverify\*(Aq and \*(Aqsimulate\*(Aq option.
.br
That means, if there is no matching SLE version for the running OS
and/or no matching system architecture in the tags of the rpm section no
rpm entries are listed during the \*(Aqverify\*(Aq and \*(Aqsimulate\*(Aq operation.
.RE
.sp
\fB[service]\fP
.RS 4
The section \fB[service]\fP is dealing with starting, enabling, disabling
and stopping services controlled by systemd.
.br
The syntax for the entries are:
.sp
\fB<servicename>=\fP\fISTRING\fP
.RS 4
where STRING is a list of valid values separated by \*(Aq\fB,\fP\*(Aq, which are
checked from left to right. The first entry of the pair \*(Aqstart\*(Aq/\*(Aqstop\*(Aq
or \*(Aqenable\*(Aq/\*(Aqdisable\*(Aq will be used as new settings for the service.
.br
Valid services are those listed by the command \fBsystemctl
list\-unit\-files\fP.
.br
Valid values are \*(Aq\fBstart\fP\*(Aq or \*(Aq\fBstop\fP\*(Aq, \*(Aq\fBenable\fP\*(Aq or \*(Aq\fBdisable\fP\*(Aq.
.RE
.sp
\fBExceptions and Warnings:\fP
.br
For the service \fBuuidd.socket\fP only \*(Aq\fBstart\fP\*(Aq is a valid value,
because the uuidd.socket service is essential for a working SAP
environment.
.br
\~
.br
Concerning \fBsysstat.service\fP please be in mind: A running \fBsysstat\fP
service can effect the system performance. But if there are real
performance trouble with the SAP system, SAP service normally orders
the \fBsysstat\fP reports collected in \fI/var/log/sa\fP.
.br
See \fBsar\fP(1), \fBsa2\fP(8), \fBsa1\fP(8) for more information.
.br
\~
.br
If a service is enabled or disabled by default or admin choice,
\fBsaptune\fP will NOT disable or enable this service, if only \fBstart\fP or
\fBstop\fP is used. In this case it will only start/stop the service. If
such a service is started by systemd during a system reboot \fBafter\fP
the start of \fBsaptune.service\fP it will be possible that a service is
stopped/running even if it was started/stopped by \fBsaptune\fP. To change
this, the service can be additional enabled or disabled by using
\fBenable\fP or \fBdisable\fP in the Note definition or Override file.
.RE
.sp
\fB[sysctl]\fP
.RS 4
The section \fB[sysctl]\fP can be used to modify kernel parameters. The
parameters available are those listed under \fI/proc/sys/\fP.
.br
Please write the section keyword \*(Aq[sysctl]\*(Aq in the first line and add
the desired tunables in \fIsysctl.conf\fP syntax.
.sp
\fBsysctl.parameter=\fP\fIVALUE\fP
.RS 4
There will be a detection of conflicting (system) \fBsysctl\fP entries.
.br
When parsing the section \fB[sysctl]\fP in the Note definition file
\fBsaptune\fP additional collects all defined sysctl settings (parameter and
value) available in \fI/etc/sysctl.conf\fP, \fI/run/sysctl.d/\fP,
\fI/etc/sysctl.d/\fP, \fI/usr/local/lib/sysctl.d/\fP, \fI/usr/lib/sysctl.d/\fP,
\fI/lib/sysctl.d/\fP, \fI/boot/\fP (list retrieved from the comment in
\fI/etc/sysctl.conf\fP and man page \fBsysctl.conf\fP(5)). When this file list
contains a directory (like \fI/etc/sysctl.d/\fP) the files located in this
directory are read too.
.br
\fBsaptune\fP will now check, if the parameters from the section \fB[sysctl]\fP
in the Note definition file are additional defined in one or more of
the (system) sysctl config files. If yes, a warning is displayed and
logged and a footnote will be prepared for the \fBsaptune verify\fP
output. The info shown is the filename, where the parameter is
additionally defined with it\(cqs value in brackets.
.br
The central \fBsaptune\fP configuration file contains a parameter
\fBSKIP_SYSCTL_FILES\fP, which contains a comma separated list of
\fIsysctl.conf\fP files or directories containing \fIsysctl.conf\fP files, which
should be excluded from this warning message and the footnote.
.br
Default is \fBSKIP_SYSCTL_FILES="/boot"\fP to skip the WARNINGS for
\fI/boot/sysctl.conf\-<kernelversion>\fP
.br
The parameter can be adapted by \fBsaptune configure SKIP_SYSCTL_FILES
VALUE\fP.
.br
\~
.br
Hint: At the moment links are not recognized. So the linked files will
be added both in the file list.
.RE
.RE
.sp
\fB[sys]\fP
.RS 4
The section \fB[sys]\fP can be used to modify parameters available under
\fI/sys/\fP, if the related file is writable.
.br
The syntax for the sys parameters is following the \fIsysctl.conf\fP syntax.
So it\(cqs the absolute filename without the prefixed \fI/sys/\fP and all
remaining \*(Aq/\*(Aq exchanged by \*(Aq.\*(Aq
.br
e.g. \fI/sys/module/watchdog/parameters/open_timeout\fP should be written as
\fBmodule.watchdog.parameters.open_timeout\fP
.sp
\fBsys.parameter=\fP\fIVALUE\fP
.RS 4
ATTENTION: \fBsaptune\fP is NOT validating the value before trying to apply.
.RE
.RE
.sp
\fB[vm]\fP
.RS 4
The section \fB[vm]\fP manipulates \fI/sys/kernel/mm\fP switches.
.br
This section can contain the following options:
.sp
\fBTHP=\fP\fISTRING\fP
.RS 4
This option configures transparent hugepages by changing
\fI/sys/kernel/mm/transparent_hugepage/enabled\fP
.br
Possible values are \fBnever\fP to disable, \fBalways\fP to enable and
\fBmadvise\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fBKSM=\fP\fIINT\fP
.RS 4
Kernel Samepage Merging (KSM). KSM allows for an application to
register with the kernel so as to have its memory pages merged with
other processes that also register to have their pages merged. For KVM
the KSM mechanism allows for guest virtual machines to share pages
with each other. In today\(cqs environment where many of the guest
operating systems like XEN, KVM are similar and are running on same
host machine, this can result in significant memory savings, the
default value is set to 0.
.RE
.RE
.SH "TAGGING"
.sp
It is possible to limit the scope of a section (e.g. to a special
\fIhardware architecture\fP and/or a special \fIos version\fP, a special \fIblock
device hardware\fP or a \fIcloud service provider\fP) by adding \fBtags\fP to the
section definition.
.br
Tags are separated by \*(Aq:\*(Aq and can appear in any order. Unsupported tags
are considered an error and the whole section is ignored.
.br
It is possible to use more than one tag per section. The tags are
concatenated by \fBAND\fP and not OR. That means using the same tag multiple
times with different values inside a section will not work. The whole
section will be ignored.
.br
In such a case split your section into 2 (or more) consecutive
sections.
.br
For some tags the value of the tag is treated as a string and used as a
regular expression to match the content of the respective source. This
is mentioned in the description of the possible tags below. As a
reference \c
.URL "https://golang.org/pkg/regexp/#MatchString" "" ""
can be used to see,
what additional expressions may be possible inside the tag value. But
attention, only some basic ones are really supported (like \*(Aqsd[ab]\*(Aq for
block devices).
.sp
Attention: as the IBM Power platform (hardware architecture \*(Aqppc64le\*(Aq)
does not support files in \fI/sys/class/dmi\fP (the directory is not
available on this hardware architecture) some of the tags listed below
are not usable on Power systems for now.
.sp
The \fBsyntax\fP for such sections is:
.sp
[section_name:[tag=value]...]
.sp
Supported tags are:
.sp
\fBos=\fP\fI<os_version>\fP
.RS 4
to define a special \fIos version\fP
.br
Valid values for \fBos=\fP must match the value from the \fBVERSION=\fP line
of \fI/etc/os\-release\fP, e.g. 12\-SP5, 12, 15, 15\-SP5 or 16.0
.br
\~
.br
If \fBVERSION=\fP contains spaces, only the string until the first space
is used for comparison. This excludes any suffixes \- like for beta
versions \- from the comparison.
.br
\~
.br
To mark an entire major release the wildcard \fB*\fP in combination with a
separator \fB\-\fP or \fB.\fP (regardless of the one present in \fBVERSION\fP) can
be used. Valid patterns are: 12\-*, 12.*, 15\-*, 15.*, 16\-* or 16.*
.br
\~
.br
Additional there is the possibility to use a list or a range of valid
service packs or dot releases. Again the separator can be \fB\-\fP or \fB.\fP
(regardless of the one present in \fBVERSION\fP). And for service packs
(in 12/15) the \fISP\fP prefix should be omitted.
.br
e.g 15\-[2,4\-5,6\-,\-7] or 15.[2,4\-5,6\-,\-7]
.RE
.sp
\fBarch=\fP\fI<hardware_architecture>\fP
.RS 4
to define a special \fIhardware architecture\fP:
.br
Valid values for \fBarch=\fP are the output from \fBuname \-i\fP
.br
\~
.br
Example:
.br
[sysctl:os=15\-SP1:arch=ppc64le]
.RE
.sp
\fBkernel=\fP\fI<kernel_release>\fP
.RS 4
to define a special \fIkernel release\fP of the running kernel:
.br
Valid values for \fBkernel=\fP are regular expressions (RE2) that match
the output from \fBuname \-r\fP
.br
\~
.br
Example:
.br
[grub:os=15\-SP7:kernel=^6.4.0\-150700.5(1|3.[36])\-default$]
.br
[grub:kernel=^6.4.0\-150600.23.(42|47|50|53|60|78)\-default$]
.RE
.sp
\fBpmu_name=\fP\fI<CPU platform>\fP
.RS 4
to select a special \fICPU platform\fP:
.br
Valid values for \fBpmu_name=\fP are the content from
\fI/sys/devices/cpu/caps/pmu_name\fP. So this tag is an Intel CPU only
feature for now.
.br
The tag is \fBnot\fP limited to the cpu section, it can be used in any
section
.br
\~
.br
Example:
.br
[cpu:pmu_name=skylake]
.br
or
.br
[grub:pmu_name=haswell]
.RE
.sp
\fBvendor=\fP\fI<hardware vendor>\fP
.RS 4
to define a special \fIsystem hardware vendor\fP
.br
Valid values for \fBvendor=\fP are the values found in
\fI/sys/class/dmi/id/board_vendor\fP or parts of the string available in
this file.
.br
The value of the vendor tag is used as a regular expression
\fB.*<value>.*\fP to match the content of the vendor file.
.br
\~
.br
For IBM Power platform (hardware architecture \*(Aqppc64le\*(Aq) the value is
set to \*(AqIBM\*(Aq.
.RE
.sp
\fBmodel=\fP\fI<hardware model>\fP
.RS 4
to define a special \fIsystem hardware model\fP
.br
Valid values for \fBmodel=\fP are the values found in
\fI/sys/class/dmi/id/product_name\fP or parts of the string available in
this file.
.br
The value of the model tag is used as a regular expression
\fB.*<value>.*\fP to match the content of the model file.
.br
\~
.br
On IBM Power platform (hardware architecture \*(Aqppc64le\*(Aq) the file
\fI/sys/firmware/devicetree/base/model\fP is used to identify the hardware
model.
.br
\~
.br
Example:
.br
[sysctl:vendor=FUJITSU:model=RX4770 M.*]
.RE
.sp
\fBcsp=\fP\fI<cloud service provider%instance type>\fP
.RS 4
to define a special \fIcloud service provider\fP and (optional) a special
instance type (separated by \*(Aq%\*(Aq)
.br
Valid values for the cloud service provider in \fBcsp=\fP are \fBazure\fP,
\fBaws\fP, \fBgoogle\fP and \fBibmVPC\fP
.br
For the valid instance types please refer to the documentation of the
related cloud service provider.
.br
\~
.br
Example:
.br
[sysctl:csp=azure]
.br
or
.br
[sysctl:csp=google%e2\-micro]
.RE
.sp
\fBvirt=\fP\fI<virtualization type>\fP
.RS 4
to define a special \fIvirtualization\fP class or type
.br
Valid class values for \fBvirt=\fP are \fBvm\fP, \fBchroot\fP, \fBcontainer\fP and
\fBbare\-metal\fP.
.br
Valid type values for \fBvirt=\fP are the output reported by
\fB/usr/bin/systemd\-detect\-virt\fP. If no virtualization is detected it\(cqs
considered as \fBbare\-metal\fP
.br
\~
.br
Example:
.br
[sysctl:virt=vm]
.RE
.sp
\fBDMI interface tag:\fP\fI<filename>=<file content>\fP
.RS 4
Additional every filename from \fI/sys/class/dmi/id/\fP can be used as a
tag.
.br
This way it\(cqs possible to restrict a section to a special hardware
vendor or hardware model regarding the bios, the chassis, the board or
anything else provided by the DMI interface.
.br
The value of the DMI interface tag is used as a regular expression
\fB.*<value>.*\fP to match the content of the file in
\fI/sys/class/dmi/id/<filename>\fP.
.br
\~
.br
Attention: if the pattern does not match any filename in
\fI/sys/class/dmi/id\fP, the section will be skipped as unknown.
.br
\~
.br
Attention: not usable on IBM Power platform (hardware architecture
\*(Aqppc64le\*(Aq)
.br
\~
.br
Example:
.br
[sysctl:bios_vendor=Dell]
.RE
.sp
For the following 3 tags we have special rules.
.br
These tags have different effects regarding the section they are used. In
all sections except the \fB[block]\fP section they are used the same way all
other tags are used to restrict the section to be only valid on a system
which includes this special sort of block device.
.br
But used in the \fB[block]\fP section they mean that all the settings
available in this section will \fBonly\fP apply to block devices, which
match the tag value. So you can restrict the settings to dedicated block
devices.
.sp
\fBblkvendor=\fP\fI<block device hardware vendor>\fP
.RS 4
to define a \fIhardware vendor\fP to match a special block device
.br
Valid values for \fBblkvendor=\fP are the values found in
\fB/sys/block/<dev>/device/vendor\fP or parts of the string available in
this file.
.br
The value of the blkvendor tag is used as a regular expression
\fB.*<value>.*\fP to match the content of the vendor file.
.br
\~
.br
Example:
.br
[block:blkvendor=ATA]
.br
[block:blkvendor=FTS:blkmodel=PRAID EP420.*]
.br
[block:blkvendor=FUJITSU:blkmodel=ETERNUS_]
.RE
.sp
\fBblkmodel=\fP\fI<block device hardware model>\fP
.RS 4
to define a \fIhardware model\fP to match a special block device
.br
Valid values for \fBblkmodel=\fP are the values found in
\fB/sys/block/<dev>/device/model\fP or parts of the string available in
this file.
.br
The value of the \fBblkmodel\fP tag is used as a regular expression
\fB.*<value>.\fP* to match the content of the model file.
.br
\~
.br
Attention: if the tag does not match any block device, no block device
will be handled by \fBsaptune\fP. e.g. \*(Aq\fBsaptune note verify\fP\*(Aq will list NO
block devices.
.br
\~
.br
Example:
.br
[block:blkmodel=QEMU HARDDISK]
.RE
.sp
\fBblkpat=\fP\fI<pattern>\fP
.RS 4
to define a \fIpattern\fP to match a special block device in \fI/sys/block/\fP
.br
\~
.br
Attention: if the pattern does not match any block device, no block
device will be handled by \fBsaptune\fP. e.g. \fBsaptune note verify\fP will
list NO block device. There will be NO Warning or Error message logged
in such a case.
.br
\~
.br
Example:
.br
[block:blkpat=nvme] to match all \fI/sys/block/nvme.*\fP block devices
.br
[block:blkpat=sd[ab]] to match \fI/sys/block/sda\fP and \fI/sys/block/sdb\fP
.RE
.sp
For processing a section the following rules apply:
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
Only sections that match the system are processed. Sections without a
tag are always used.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
The order of the section within the file matter. Each section and
each line in a section gets processed from top to down.
.RE
.sp
The rules apply for shipped Note definition files as well as for
customer defined Note definition files. Tagged sections can be used in
override files.
.sp
\fBATTENTION:\fP To be clear \- if there are more sections with the \fBsame\fP
\fIsection_name\fP containing the \fBsame\fP \fIparameters\fP with \fBdifferent\fP
\fIvalues\fP, the last valid section will win.
.sp
So it\(cqs all about \fBorder\fP.
.sp
The following section definitions are available and used in the \fBsaptune\fP
SAP Note definition files. Each of these sections can be used in a
vendor or customer specific Note definition file placed in
\fI/etc/saptune/extra\fP.
.sp
List of supported sections:
.br
\fBversion\fP, \fBblock\fP, \fBcpu\fP, \fBfilesystem\fP, \fBgrub\fP, \fBlimits\fP, \fBlogin\fP, \fBmem\fP, \fBpagecache\fP,
\fBreminder\fP, \fBrpm\fP, \fBservice\fP, \fBsysctl\fP, \fBsys\fP, \fBvm\fP
.SH "FILES"
.sp
\fI/usr/share/saptune/notes\fP
.RS 3
.ll -.6i
.sp
The directory (Package Area) contains the Notes, which are shipped by
the saptune RPM package. They are not directly used by \fBsaptune\fP but get
copied during installation or update to the Working or Staging Area.
.br
Do not change the files located here. You will lose all your changes
during a \fBsaptune\fP package update. Use an override or extra file for your
changes as described in \fBsaptune\fP(8).
.br
.RE
.ll
.sp
\fI/var/lib/saptune/working/notes\fP
.RS 3
.ll -.6i
.sp
The directory (the Working Area) contains Notes, which are shipped by
the \fBsaptune\fP RPM package. They are copied from the Package Area during
installation or update if they do not belong to the Staging Area due to
the configuration.
.br
Do not change the files located here. Depending on your configuration
you will lose all your changes during a \fBsaptune\fP package update or a
\*(Aq\fBsaptune staging release\fP\*(Aq command later. Use an override or extra file
for your changes as described in \fBsaptune\fP(8).
.br
.RE
.ll
.sp
\fI/var/lib/saptune/staging/latest\fP
.RS 3
.ll -.6i
.sp
The directory (the Staging Area) contains Notes coming from the package.
They are copied from the Package Area during installation or update if
they do not belong to the Working Area due to the configuration.
.br
Do not change the files located here. You will lose all your changes
during a \fBsaptune\fP package update. Use an override or extra file for your
changes as described in \fBsaptune\fP(8).
.br
.RE
.ll
.sp
\fI/etc/saptune/extra\fP
.RS 3
.ll -.6i
.sp
The directory contains vendor or customer specific Notes created by
\fBsaptune note create NOTEID\fP
.br
.RE
.ll
.sp
\fI/etc/saptune/override\fP
.RS 3
.ll -.6i
.sp
The directory contains overrides for Notes created by \fBsaptune note
customise NOTEID\fP
.br
.RE
.ll
